{"name":"Aloe Essence","hex":"#ecf1e2","fetched":"2026-10-21","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aloe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The resins of the tree Aquilaria malaccensis (syn. Aquilaria agallocha), known for their fragrant aroma, produced after infection by the fungus Phialophora parasitica.","Any plant of the large and variable genus Aloe."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aloe"},{"term":"essence","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The inherent nature of a thing or idea.","The true nature of anything, not accidental or illusory."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/essence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aloe","blocks":[{"text":"Aloe is a genus containing 594 species of flowering succulent plants. The most widely known species is Aloe vera, or \"true aloe\", which is cultivated as a source for topical uses and dietary supplements. Other species, such as Aloe ferox, are also cultivated or harvested from the wild for similar applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg/330px-Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aloe","wikidata":"Q127134"},{"title":"Essence","blocks":[{"text":"Essence has various meanings and uses for different thinkers and in different contexts. It is used in philosophy and theology as a designation for the property or set of properties or attributes that make an entity the entity it is or, expressed negatively, without which it would lose its identity. Essence is contrasted with accident, which is a property or attribute the entity has accidentally or contingently, but upon which its identity does not depend."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Essence","wikidata":"Q190995"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg/500px-Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg/500px-Aloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_%284387600468%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAloe_arborescens_on_Monte_Vumba_(4387600468).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/500px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/1280px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Split_Aloe.jpg/500px-Split_Aloe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Succulent plants, such as aloe, store water in their enlarged fleshy leaves","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASplit_Aloe.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg/500px-Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg/1280px-Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg/500px-Aloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spikes on an Aloe vera houseplant","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAloe_Vera_houseplant.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Aloe_diversity.jpg/500px-Aloe_diversity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia